1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:02,768
You have been told this your whole life.

2
00:00:03,495 --> 00:00:04,437
Muscle cramp?

3
00:00:05,164 --> 00:00:06,107
Eat a banana.

4
00:00:06,834 --> 00:00:08,157
Potassium fixes it.

5
00:00:08,884 --> 00:00:10,780
Let me take that away first.

6
00:00:11,506 --> 00:00:15,499
The clinical evidence behind it is far weaker than you think.

7
00:00:16,226 --> 00:00:22,189
And what a banana actually does inside a muscle cell is much more interesting than the myth.

8
00:00:22,916 --> 00:00:27,989
A medium banana carries about four hundred and twenty milligrams of potassium.

9
00:00:28,715 --> 00:00:32,772
That sounds like a lot, until you learn where potassium lives.

10
00:00:33,499 --> 00:00:40,414
Roughly ninety eight percent of the potassium in your body is already inside your cells, not in your blood.

11
00:00:41,141 --> 00:00:46,341
Your blood holds a tiny fraction, and your body defends that number ferociously.

12
00:00:47,068 --> 00:00:49,917
So a banana is not refilling an empty tank.

13
00:00:50,644 --> 00:00:52,476
Something else is going on.

14
00:00:53,203 --> 00:00:54,463
Chewing starts it.

15
00:00:55,190 --> 00:01:02,106
Salivary amylase starts breaking down starch immediately, and how much starch there is depends on ripeness.

16
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:08,033
A green banana is mostly resistant starch, which your own enzymes cannot digest.

17
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:13,832
As it ripens, enzymes inside the fruit convert that starch into simple sugars.

18
00:01:14,559 --> 00:01:18,806
A spotted banana is a completely different fuel from a green one.

19
00:01:19,533 --> 00:01:28,228
In the small intestine, potassium crosses mostly by simple diffusion, while glucose is pulled across by a sodium dependent transporter.

20
00:01:28,955 --> 00:01:34,981
Both enter the blood, and within about an hour that potassium starts moving where it matters.

21
00:01:35,708 --> 00:01:37,286
Into your muscle cells.

22
00:01:38,013 --> 00:01:40,989
And here is the machine that makes it matter.

23
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:47,361
Embedded in every muscle cell membrane sits a protein called the sodium potassium pump.

24
00:01:48,088 --> 00:01:50,237
It does one thing, relentlessly.

25
00:01:50,964 --> 00:01:55,910
It pushes three sodium ions out of the cell and pulls two potassium ions in.

26
00:01:56,637 --> 00:01:57,262
Out, in.

27
00:01:57,988 --> 00:01:59,249
Millions of times.

28
00:01:59,975 --> 00:02:01,553
This pump is expensive.

29
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:06,845
At rest it consumes around a fifth of all the energy your body spends.

30
00:02:07,572 --> 00:02:12,772
Your cells burn that much fuel just to keep sodium outside and potassium inside.

31
00:02:13,499 --> 00:02:14,822
Why pay that price?

32
00:02:15,549 --> 00:02:17,953
Because that imbalance is a battery.

33
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:26,676
More potassium inside, more sodium outside, and the interior of your muscle cell sits at roughly ninety millivolts negative.

34
00:02:27,403 --> 00:02:31,777
That charged, ready state is called the resting membrane potential.

35
00:02:32,504 --> 00:02:34,145
Your muscle is not idle.

36
00:02:34,872 --> 00:02:35,814
It is loaded.

37
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:38,373
Now a nerve signal arrives.

38
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:43,601
Sodium channels fly open, sodium floods in, and the charge collapses.

39
00:02:44,328 --> 00:02:49,147
That collapse is the action potential, the electrical command to contract.

40
00:02:49,874 --> 00:02:56,853
It races along the fiber, dives into channels called T tubules, and releases calcium from an internal store.

41
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:59,158
Calcium is the trigger.

42
00:02:59,885 --> 00:03:07,881
It binds to the contraction machinery, uncovering the sites where protein filaments grip each other, and the fiber shortens.

43
00:03:08,608 --> 00:03:09,995
That is contraction.

44
00:03:10,722 --> 00:03:13,190
But contraction is only half a cycle.

45
00:03:13,916 --> 00:03:16,384
To fire again, the cell has to reset.

46
00:03:17,111 --> 00:03:24,154
It resets by opening potassium channels, letting potassium rush out and dragging the charge back to negative.

47
00:03:24,881 --> 00:03:27,602
Potassium does not cause the contraction.

48
00:03:28,329 --> 00:03:31,369
Potassium is what makes the next one possible.

49
00:03:32,096 --> 00:03:33,737
That is the real answer.

50
00:03:34,464 --> 00:03:36,296
Potassium is not the spark.

51
00:03:37,023 --> 00:03:38,219
It is the reload.

52
00:03:38,946 --> 00:03:41,350
Meanwhile the sugar has its own job.

53
00:03:42,077 --> 00:03:49,374
Insulin signals a transporter called glute four to move to the muscle cell surface, and glucose is pulled inside.

54
00:03:50,101 --> 00:03:51,933
Some is burned immediately.

55
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:57,860
Some is chained together and stored as glycogen, your muscle's local fuel depot.

56
00:03:58,587 --> 00:04:06,710
Bananas also carry vitamin B six, which your body converts into a cofactor for the enzyme that breaks that glycogen back down.

57
00:04:07,437 --> 00:04:10,032
So what does the evidence actually say?

58
00:04:10,759 --> 00:04:13,417
On performance, it is surprisingly good.

59
00:04:14,144 --> 00:04:22,521
In controlled trials, cyclists fueled with bananas performed about the same as those using a commercial carbohydrate sports drink.

60
00:04:23,248 --> 00:04:26,923
Same power output, plus polyphenols the drink never had.

61
00:04:27,650 --> 00:04:29,863
On cramps, the picture collapses.

62
00:04:30,590 --> 00:04:39,285
Studies comparing blood electrolytes in athletes who cramp and those who do not have repeatedly failed to find a meaningful difference.

63
00:04:40,012 --> 00:04:43,115
The leading explanation today is neuromuscular.

64
00:04:43,842 --> 00:04:49,995
Fatigue distorts the reflex control of the muscle, inhibition drops away, and the muscle locks.

65
00:04:50,722 --> 00:04:54,461
And eating a banana during a cramp fails on timing alone.

66
00:04:55,187 --> 00:04:59,625
That potassium needs tens of minutes to be absorbed and distributed.

67
00:05:00,352 --> 00:05:02,311
The cramp will be over first.

68
00:05:03,038 --> 00:05:04,806
So, three practical rules.

69
00:05:05,533 --> 00:05:05,904
One.

70
00:05:06,631 --> 00:05:12,466
For fuel, eat it thirty to sixty minutes before training, and pick a ripe, spotted banana.

71
00:05:13,193 --> 00:05:17,186
The starch is already converted, so the sugar arrives faster.

72
00:05:17,913 --> 00:05:18,283
Two.

73
00:05:19,010 --> 00:05:23,320
For steadier energy, or for your gut bacteria, pick a greener one.

74
00:05:24,047 --> 00:05:28,167
That resistant starch reaches your colon intact and feeds them.

75
00:05:28,894 --> 00:05:29,391
Three.

76
00:05:30,118 --> 00:05:32,204
For cramps, stop relying on it.

77
00:05:32,931 --> 00:05:37,559
Gradual conditioning and managing fatigue do more than any single food.

78
00:05:38,286 --> 00:05:39,673
One serious warning.

79
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:51,066
If you have kidney disease, or take medication that raises potassium, your body cannot clear excess potassium normally, and high potassium foods can become dangerous.

80
00:05:51,792 --> 00:05:54,705
Speak to your doctor before increasing them.

81
00:05:55,432 --> 00:05:58,026
None of this was really about a banana.

82
00:05:58,753 --> 00:06:08,147
It was about a pump that never stops, an electrical charge sitting inside every cell you own, and the difference between the spark and the reload.

83
00:06:08,874 --> 00:06:11,532
So which molecule should we follow next?

84
00:06:12,259 --> 00:06:17,650
Write a food and an organ in the comments, and we will take it apart, cell by cell.

